const EN = `# Culture and Etiquette in Vietnam

Vietnamese people are usually warm, patient, and kind to visitors. You do not need to know every custom. Polite behavior, a calm voice, and a smile help in almost every situation. Rules and customs can change by place, so follow local signs and the advice of staff.

---

## Greetings and Respect
- A small nod and a smile is a good greeting. Handshakes are common in business and with other travelers.
- Learn a few words: **Xin chào** (hello), **Cảm ơn** (thank you), and **Xin lỗi** (sorry or excuse me).
- Age and seniority are important. Greet older people first and speak politely to them.
- Give and receive things with two hands, or with your right hand while your left hand touches your right arm. This is a sign of respect, especially with older people.
- Stay calm if there is a problem. Shouting or showing anger in public makes people lose face, and it usually makes things worse.

---

## Temples and Pagodas
- Cover your shoulders and knees. Avoid sleeveless tops, short shorts, and short skirts.
- Look at the entrance for shoes. If other people leave their shoes outside, do the same. Take off your hat.
- Speak quietly and turn your phone to silent.
- Do not touch Buddha statues, offerings, or monks. Do not take food or fruit from the altar.
- Do not point your feet at altars or statues. If there is a raised step at the door, step over it, not on it.
- Ask before taking photos inside a temple, and follow any signs that say no photos.

---

## Ho Chi Minh Mausoleum and Memorial Sites
- The Ho Chi Minh Mausoleum in Hanoi is a place of deep respect. Dress modestly, walk quietly, keep your hands out of your pockets, and do not take photos inside.
- The mausoleum is often closed on some days of the week and for yearly maintenance. Check the opening days before you go.
- Museums and memorial sites about the war are sensitive places for many people. Be quiet and respectful, especially in front of photos and exhibits.

---

## Dress Code
- In big cities, casual summer clothes are fine in most places.
- Dress more modestly when you visit temples, government offices, rural villages, and family homes.
- Swimwear is for the beach and the pool only. Do not walk around town in swimwear.

---

## Table Manners and Eating Customs
- Do not stick chopsticks upright in a rice bowl. It looks like incense for the dead.
- Do not pass food from chopsticks to chopsticks. Use the serving spoon or the clean end of your chopsticks to take food from shared plates.
- Wait for older people or the host to start eating, and offer them food first.
- It is normal to hold your bowl close to your mouth and to make some noise with noodle soup.
- At a toast, people often say **"Một, hai, ba, dô!"** (one, two, three, cheers!). You may politely decline alcohol, and you can just touch your glass to your lips.
- If you are invited to a home, take off your shoes at the door, and bring a small gift such as fruit or sweets.
- When you give flowers, avoid white flowers and chrysanthemums, because they are linked with funerals.
- In groups, the person who invites usually pays. Splitting the bill is not as common as in Western countries.

---

## Body Language and Public Behavior
- The head is considered the most respected part of the body. Do not touch the head of an adult or a child.
- Do not point with one finger. Use your whole hand, with the palm facing down.
- To call someone, hold your palm down and wave the fingers toward you. Holding your palm up and curling one finger is rude.
- Do not point your feet at people or sit with your feet up on a seat.
- Holding hands is fine. Kissing and hugging in public are not usual and may make people uncomfortable, especially outside big cities.
- Do not cross your arms or put your hands on your hips when you talk to an older person. It can look rude.

---

## Photography and Drones
- Ask before you take photos of people, especially monks, children, and people in villages. A smile and a gesture are enough.
- Do not take photos of military sites, border areas, airports, and some government buildings.
- Drones are tightly controlled in Vietnam. Check the current rules and get any permission you need before you fly one.

---

## Sensitive Topics
- Vietnam is a one-party socialist country. Politics, history, and the war can be sensitive topics. If someone starts a discussion, listen politely and avoid arguments or strong opinions.
- Show respect to the national flag and to images of Ho Chi Minh.
- Do not hand out religious or political material.

---

## Shopping and Bargaining
- Bargaining is normal in markets and with street sellers, but not in shops with fixed prices, malls, and most restaurants.
- Stay friendly and smile. Start with a lower price and move slowly to a price that both sides accept.
- It is fine to walk away politely if the price is too high.
- Do not bargain hard for very small amounts, because a small price difference often matters more to the seller than to you.
- Keep small notes for markets and street stalls, because sellers may not have change for large bills.

---

## Traffic and Street Manners
- When you cross a busy street, walk slowly and steadily. Do not run or stop suddenly. Motorbikes will go around you.
- Look and listen carefully, even at pedestrian crossings.
- A helmet is required for everyone on a motorbike. Check the Transport guide for rides and rules.
- Drivers must have a valid license that Vietnam accepts, and the limit for alcohol while driving is zero. Check the current rules before you drive.

---

## Festivals and Holidays
- **Tết (Lunar New Year)** is the biggest holiday of the year. Many shops, restaurants, and tours close for several days, transport is very crowded, and prices can go up. Book early if you travel at this time.
- During Tết, people give small red envelopes with money, called **lì xì**, for good luck.
- On public holidays, tourist places can be crowded. Plan your day in advance.

---

## Local Laws to Know
- Drug laws are very strict. Penalties can include long prison sentences, and the death penalty exists for drug trafficking.
- E-cigarettes and heated tobacco products are banned. Do not bring them.
- Smoking is not allowed in many indoor public places.
- Keep your passport or a copy with you, and follow the rules about registering where you stay.
- Do not take part in illegal gambling, and do not buy or sell items such as wild animal products.
- If you have a problem, contact your embassy, the local police, or the tourist police. Keep your travel insurance details with you.`

